A||||11||RedHaze86SD4||Stripped my 87 2.5 to the block and OWW!!!||||9||
Z||000000||RedHaze86SD4||12-10-2001||03:54 PM||||Well I got the 87 stripped down to the block and what do you know? The number 3 cylinders intake valve was apparently tapping the piston and it was run to the point where the valve broke into the chamber and shattered the piston into 3 or 4 parts. Luckily enough the rod only left a minor groove on the farthest side of the cylinder.<BR> So my question is this.. For a minor groove I mean its so minor you can barely feel it and you can only see the slight shadow of it even being there. So Whats the highest you can go for a bore? .2, .3 or .4??? just bore it over enough to clean the edges and safely run the engine still. Someone said rehone it but I dont think that would work. You have to bore it right? Anyone?? Anyways. The rest of the motor is fine. Except the head is messed up a little. the only thing I can see wrong is the spring seat for the 3 intake is busted and the spring and seat are loose. Anyideas??? I was thinking of picking up the aluminum SD4 head from a guy down here thats selling an unused one for 500. its complete and gm claims it'll blt up to the stock block and intake and exhaust manifolds... So??? Help??? Please??? <BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/redhaze1_1.GIF"> <BR>'86 SC 2M4 (RedHaze)<BR>'87 Coupe (Streetin' soon?)<BR>'83 F250 w/460 (Big Beefy) :D||||reg||9
Z||000001||fierohoho||12-10-2001||04:01 PM||||You have it torn down that far I would have it bored .030 over and put in new .030 over pistons and have the crank polished and get bearings to match the polish.<P>Bring the .030 over pistons with when you have then block bored so they can measure them and bore the block to their size and have them mark the pistons for each cyclinder.<P>Might as well start fresh.<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/lightbar.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||erikred||12-10-2001||04:08 PM||||Go far the SD head! $500 is a good price and then you can tell other members (like me) what it was like on your Duke. Don't bother getting the head unless you get a good cam though. <P>If you are going through the trouble of boring the engine, you may as well rebuild it, and if you are going through the trouble of rebuilding the engine you may as well put some goodies in it. Stronger rods and forged pistons would go well with your new cam and head. Otherwise just hone it out, you can do it with the engine in the car, and bolt a replacement stock head (with some mods maybe) back on.<P>Just my oppinion.<P>Erik-||||reg||14
Z||000003||RedHaze86SD4||12-10-2001||04:26 PM||||Well at the moment I'm looking at boring it .20 over using the SD4 head, a comp cam with performance rebuild kit, not really performance but thats what it says. Some rods from APR and someone said lunati has good rods that can fit.. no idea need to call them.. Was thinking of the sd4 intake from gm but it only excepts 4brl and I want the holley tpi unit. Also am talking to a guy about ordering the hooker header for me with a low torque cat. Think its keeps the back pressure so the duke gains or keeps low end power. also have a guy whos almost done with my flowmaster exhaust. for the 86.  looks like its going on the 87 instead.||||reg||1
Z||000004||tesmith66||12-10-2001||04:46 PM||||Take your intake & exhaust manifolds and your old head gasket with you when you look at the SD4 head to make sure they fit. Your best bet on the block is to bore it. That way you'll know the bores are round and the block is in good shape. It costs more and is a lot of work pulling the engine, but when it's all said and done, it'll go another 100,000+ miles! It's nice to not have to go back in there for a while!<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000005||avengador1||12-10-2001||06:47 PM||||I hope you mean bore it .020" or twenty thousands of an inch not .20" or twenty hundreds of an inch. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Freshj||12-10-2001||09:33 PM||||Wouldn't it be easier to say two tenths instead of twenty hundreds? ||||reg||5
Z||000007||Fierowrecker||12-10-2001||09:48 PM||||Hey Red!<BR>Why not save the overall cost of building a Puke?<BR>Buy an Olds 3800 series I and triple the output...<BR>If you buy a used CAR with all the goodies, then you get the ECM and wiring harness too...<BR>I paid $200 for a running Olds with the 3800, which is $300 cheaper than the SD head, and it is built to handle the HP, where the Puke will crumble like tissue paper...<P>------------------<BR>crash... The Fierowrecker<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/uploads/crash_sig.jpg">          <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Fierowrecker (edited 12-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||ITB 2M4||12-11-2001||12:04 AM||||From what I've heard, the SD head will only fit the SD block.  I used to work for a GM dealer, and seem to remember reading that in the GM Performance Catalog.<P>BTW, I've balanced and blueprinted my 2.5 as well, and it easily turns 5500rpm.  I've had the flywheel trued up as well, but due to class restrictions, I can't lighten it any.  Taking a couple of lbs. off the flywheel would be good for the crank, but you might notice a difference pulling away from stops the more you take off the flywheel.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4<BR>AOL IM- DGPFiero||||reg||1
Z||000009||Gerald||12-11-2001||12:24 AM||||Is it just me or does fierowrecker always jump into every duke topic and start talking about expensive, time consuming swaps. Also it would be nice if he didn't keep referring to my engine as a "puke".<BR> Not trying to start a flame war here, just think he should chill a little. The majority of fieros on the road are dukes and not everyone wants to swap.<P>As for RedHaze question: the 87/88 intake,tbi,exhaust manifold,head all have different bolt patterns then the 84-86 dukes. The holly tbi will not bolt to the 87 intake and the 87 intake will not bolt to the sd head. Some people have made adapters but I don't know how they turned out. If you can make it work I'm sure it will be worth the effort.||||reg||1
Z||000010||maryjane||12-11-2001||01:07 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Freshj:<BR><B>Wouldn't it be easier to say two tenths instead of twenty hundreds? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>No, .20" is referred to as 'two hundred thousandths. Michrometers are calibrated in thousanths, not tenths of an inch. There is usually a legend at the bottom of a print that shows what the difference is in the allowable tolerance. How far the decimal place is carried determines the tolerance. Depends on who drew the print and who the customer is. If there is no 'called' tolerance on the part itself, you use the legend. It might look like this:<BR>0    +-.010<BR>00   +-.005<BR>000  +-.002<BR>The further the decimal (more zeros shown) the less error allowed.<BR>Actually, in precision machine shops, a 'tenth' is referring to 1 ten thousandth, or '.0001'. For instance, a 1/2" machined pin might appear on a drawing with a 'called' dimension of .500 (+.0001,-0), which means it has a tolerance of one ten thousanths oversize-and no tolerance undersize. A machinist would say this as "five hundred thousandths, plus a tenth, minus nothin". A very slight press fit.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||12-11-2001||07:22 PM||||The block likely needs boring. not just for the scratch. It's pobably barrel shapped or out of round.<P>How much they require won't be known until the bores are all measured. The valve scratch isn't the only factor.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
